{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792920926,"nonce":"l13mqKhbS+GA1/u/uavzNHoXj/4=","message":"Implemented locally in 4b8c042675cc24b310bc54c02f85374019ddb9b1.\n\nNative Div/Canvas subtrees use paired node-id brackets across the background and foreground visits. The producer excludes captured group alpha from both child streams. Batch normalization joins foreground content at its group's background paint anchor, retaining local BG-before-FG ordering and leaving independent foreground in the later pass. Nested node/local layers, native primitive/path geometry, raw RGBA images, clip rows and image/command cursors survive rebasing. Drains, merges, foreground projection, clear/reuse, borrowed ordered dispatch and tight subtree bounds share the normalization contract. Large raw RGBA payloads move during draining normalization rather than being copied; borrowed unresolved dispatch clones its source.\n\nMoving native ancestors open a unit-opacity shared boundary when needed, so their own background cannot be dispatched after a nested captured child. Native moving captures retain static paint positions and suppress independent descendant promotion. Motion updates, including settled writes, and CSS cache updates reject patches that use pre-join ranges and repaint. Unsupported moving owners and global overlays cannot pull native children out of their original later pass. Collected text/SVG/ImageElement and Div background images, glass, effects/blends and 3D boundaries preserve existing fallback. Canvas custom GPU passes and meshes and named Sample are outside this fix.\n\nNegative evidence: original native overlap produced [64,128,64,255], expected [0,128,128,255]. A first join fixed alpha but dispatched moving capture after a later yellow background sibling. The moving-ancestor regression then produced opaque red [255,0,0,255], expected [128,128,0,255]; the unsupported moving-owner fixture also wrongly captured its native child. Both additional negatives are fixed. Solid path fills use primitive mesh payloads, so the expanded fixture uses a tinted native path brush to exercise actual path vertices/indices as well. An unprivileged offscreen run could not access the GPU adapter and was rerun with native GPU access.\n\nNormal checks pass: nine new cross-stream cases, 30 adjacent app controls (seven opacity, ten foreground, seven motion and six retained-canvas), and 19 GPU controls (seven producer-opacity, four auxiliary routing, six ordered-overlay and two layer-stack). All nine new cases also pass with both storage capabilities forced off (data-texture mode); capability selection was restored without a renderer.rs diff. Touched-file rustfmt and diff checks pass. All four affected crate CHANGELOGs are updated. CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2, capacities 1024; no source edits while builds/probes ran. No workspace/DSL/docs/platform or unrelated gates. No source or tracker push.\n\nCosts: one eligibility traversal and candidate-id storage per full paint, transient native command AST/maps and geometry copies only when captures are resolved, shared brackets during the producer visits, and existing tight pooled targets for partial-opacity composition. Moving captures use conservative full repaint, including when another motion binding shares the same cached batch. Static frames can reuse cache. No shader, GPU primitive/ClipPath ABI or new persistent render-target system. No timing, allocator or device-wide benchmark claim. Parent remains open for collected content, unsupported mixed ownership, separate GPU passes/meshes and named Sample. Secondary-window queue/binding isolation remains 3d90a31.\n\nLogs: /private/tmp/blinc-cross-stream-{negative,ancestor-negative,final-normal,dt-cross_stream_group}.log and the app-{opacity,foreground,motion,canvas}/gpu-{producer,aux,overlay,layer-stack,nested-layers} logs. Drivers: /private/tmp/blinc_cross_stream_gates.py and /private/tmp/blinc_cross_stream_modes.py. Main is five commits ahead of origin/main; no push.","files":null}]}